my sample JSON had acc_aBc12 in source_acc_id instead of target_acc_id, which would subtract ₦250 twice instead of refunding it.
How I'm fixing it:
• Swap source_acc_id and target_acc_id on reversals: A REVERSAL will mirror the original transaction by flipping its account fields so money flows in the exact opposite direction:
	• Reversing an expense/withdrawal (source = A, target = null) creates a reversal with source = null, target = A (money flows back into A).
	• Reversing income/deposit (source = null, target = A) creates a reversal with source = A, target = null (money flows out of A).
	• Reversing a transfer (source = A, target = B) creates a reversal with source = B, target = A (money flows from B back to A).
• Why this is cleaner: Every transaction row is self-contained. Balance calculation uses one consistent rule (credits where target_acc_id == acc minus debits where source_acc_id == acc) without needing to look up parent transactions, while reverses_transaction_id enforces the "reverse at most once" rule and maintains the audit link.
Corrected sample JSON:
json
{
  "id": "trx_8kLm2",
  "amount": 25000,
  "trx_type": "REVERSAL",
  "category": null,
  "desc": "Correction of incorrectly recorded expense",
  "source_acc_id": null,
  "target_acc_id": "acc_aBc12",
  "transaction_date": "2026-11-11",
  "reverses_transaction_id": "trx_123Df"
}



Applying current_balance - reversal_amount >= 0 to every reversal would wrongly block a user at ₦0 from reversing an expense that adds money back.
Updated reversal check rule (based on direction of funds):
Only the account losing money (source_acc_id on the reversal) needs a balance check:
• Reversing an expense / withdrawal (source = null, target = A): Money flows into A. No balance check is needed—only verify that A is active and the original transaction hasn't been reversed yet.
• Reversing income / deposit (source = A, target = null): Money flows out of A. Require A to be active and balance(A) - amount >= 0.
• Reversing a transfer (source = B, target = A): Money flows out of B and into A. Require both A and B to be active, and require balance(B) - amount >= 0 (the recipient of the original transfer must still have enough funds to send back).
In short: whenever a reversal has a source_acc_id (an outflow), check that source_acc_id has enough balance; when source_acc_id is null (an inflow), skip the balance check.



I'll define all three startup cases explicitly so a failed load can never lead to data loss.
How startup handles each case:
1. No storage file exists (first run): Initialize with a clean empty state (accounts = [], transactions = []) and write .storage.json atomically on the first save (or initial setup).
2. Valid storage file exists: Delete any stale .storage.json.tmp (with a warning), parse .storage.json, and validate all records via Account.from_dict() and Transaction.from_dict() into memory.
3. Unreadable or invalid file (corrupted JSON, OS permission error, or invalid record data): Fail fast and halt startup with a clear error message telling the user the file cannot be read or is corrupted. Never fall back to an empty in-memory state.
The one outcome that must be impossible:
• Overwriting a broken/unreadable .storage.json with an empty state on the next save. Because our persistence layer rewrites the whole file on every save, falling back to an empty list after a failed load would permanently wipe the user's real file the next time they save anything. Halting on load failure makes that impossible.



my implementation uses a strict chronological running-balance rule for BR-10. Here is how that applies to each point:
• BR-10 ("Balance as of when?"): BR-10 simulates the chronological running balance sorted by (transaction_date, insertion_order) with the new transaction included, and rejects the write if the running balance drops below ₦0 at any point in time.
	• In your scenario: Recording a ₦5,000 expense dated Nov 10 when Nov 10's balance was ₦0 is rejected, because the account would have a -₦5,000 balance from Nov 10 until the Nov 15 income arrives.
	• Why: This guarantees the ledger never models spending money before the account actually had funds on that date.
• Could our app produce that sample file? No—if the sample is ordered chronologically, having an expense appear before the income that funds it would violate BR-10 (since the balance would go negative first). I will fix the sample data so the funding income is dated on or before the expense.
• Does list position mean anything? In .storage.json, list position preserves insertion order (when each record was added). Final total balance doesn't depend on list order, but running-balance checks use insertion order as a tie-breaker.
• Same-date ordering: Transactions on the same transaction_date are ordered by insertion order (their position in the append-only list).
• Should the loader re-check balances? No—the loader validates schema and data integrity via from_dict() (valid types, statuses, positive amounts, and valid IDs), while BR-10 is enforced on every write. Blocking startup on business-rule balance checks could lock a user out of their file instead of letting them inspect or reverse a bad entry.



 I'll add strict field validation to Transaction, remove the duplicate account ID in JSON, and define ID collision handling:

1. Required vs. Null Fields by Transaction Type


| trx_type | source_acc_id | target_acc_id | reverses_transaction_id | Extra Invariant |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| INCOME | null | Required | null | amount > 0 |
| EXPENSE | Required | null | null | amount > 0 |
| TRANSFER | Required | Required | null | source_acc_id != target_acc_id, amount > 0 |
| REVERSAL | Set if reversing INCOME or TRANSFER; else null | Set if reversing EXPENSE or TRANSFER; else null | Required | At least one account set; source != target; amount > 0 |


2. Where Enforcement Lives

• Single-record invariants (above table): Enforced inside the Transaction model (__post_init__ / from_dict() in models/), so an invalid Transaction can never be constructed in memory or loaded from JSON.
• Cross-record invariants (checking that referenced accounts exist/are active, or that reverses_transaction_id matches the original amount and hasn't been reversed twice): Enforced in services/.

3. Duplicate Account IDs & ID Collisions

• Removing duplicate IDs in JSON: I will store "accounts" as a JSON list ([ {"id": "acc_...", ...} ]) so each account ID appears only once, and index it into a dict in memory on load (failing startup if duplicate IDs exist in the file).
• ID generation & collisions: IDs are generated using random tokens (secrets.token_urlsafe  with acc_ / trx_ prefixes). Before inserting into the in-memory dict, the service checks if new_id in store and regenerates a new ID on collision so an existing account or transaction can never be overwritten.